{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-78680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/assets/14d1fc5a92c28cc7f84c1ec23929701f1aca67b1a15d6113fa83f1428354fc11.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,-1)$ with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $3,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,-1)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,-1),$ $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd2e5e871b6c74e0b963a0c4153f5795d89d5cab6bb61fa8d50bed50b80946ba"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1"] ["math" "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1"] ["math" "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1"] ["math" "x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1"] ["math" "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-79278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/assets/4e9218dcfc77ec080139f2650ea19f78199557e0ce2d8d53c1996ec0ea6145a6.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-79278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/assets/dfbe843a9ad0847d7174984fa7c7c48834af48b3ee2335b5383b15f7fb826763.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\pi ,2\\pi ]$, we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-79278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd2e5e871b6c74e0b963a0c4153f5795d89d5cab6bb61fa8d50bed50b80946ba"} {"question" "q-79278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-79278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-79278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-106405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-106405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-106405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd2e5e871b6c74e0b963a0c4153f5795d89d5cab6bb61fa8d50bed50b80946ba"} {"question" "q-106405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-106405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-106405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-106711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/assets/8498bb189b51954a79f8ca6638631d6acd0a3d4fe86f6f4cecb2de77ee370f94.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-106711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/assets/fb686f7295c2c6ac3058361ecc0438ae56d650950833185b17ec959d654cc511.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-106711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd2e5e871b6c74e0b963a0c4153f5795d89d5cab6bb61fa8d50bed50b80946ba"} {"question" "q-106711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-106711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-106711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-327811" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-327811" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$  We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{72} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =11\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{11} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{11}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{121} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-327811" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd2e5e871b6c74e0b963a0c4153f5795d89d5cab6bb61fa8d50bed50b80946ba"} {"question" "q-327811" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"} {"question" "q-327811" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x^2}{72}+\\frac{y^2}{121}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "68264cf25a7df6508a095fa6d82608f58a80942dfc7bebb2588e2aa7a345f932"}] "usage" {"q-79278" [] "q-78680" []} "basis" 1219 "retractions" [[17592186152717 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2746/Source/Images/q-78680.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,-1)$ with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $3$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta  \\in  [0, 2\\pi ),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,-1)$?"] [17592186152724 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2746/Source/Images/q-79278.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 3\\cos \\theta , y = 2\\sin \\theta $.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta $?"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186151213 17592186151214 17592186151215 17592186151216 17592186151217 17592186151255 17592186151256 17592186151257 17592186151258 17592186151259] "immutable_fields" [[17592186151212 :answer-field/correct] [17592186151212 :answer-field/id] [17592186151212 :answer-field/key] [17592186151212 :answer-field/type] [1759218615